Since $-1 \le x \le 1,$ there exists an angle $\theta,$ $0^\circ \le \theta \le 180^\circ,$ such that $\cos \theta = x.$  Then $\sqrt{1 - x^2} = \sin \theta.$

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair O, X, Y, Z;

O = (0,0);
X = (10,0);
Y = (0,3);
Z = 7*dir(40);

draw(O--X--Z--Y--cycle);
draw(O--Z);

label("$O$", O, SW);
label("$X$", X, E);
label("$Y$", Y, NW);
label("$Z$", Z, N);
label("$10$", (O + X)/2, S);
label("$3$", (O + Y)/2, W);
label("$7$",(O + Z)/2, SE);
label("$90^\circ - \theta$", (1.5,0.4));
